        {
          "annotations": [],
          "text": "It can, yes—especially if the claim is being presented without clear evidence, testable predictions, or a way it could be proven wrong.\n\nA useful distinction is:\n\n- **Scientific hypothesis:** speculative at first, but testable, evidence-based, and open to revision.\n- **Pure speculation:** plausible-sounding, but not clearly testable or supported by data.\n- **Pseudoscience:** presented as scientific while avoiding falsification, cherry-picking evidence, or relying mainly on authority/anecdotes.\n\nSo if something is mostly “could be,” “might imply,” or “some experts think,” without concrete data or testable mechanisms, skepticism is reasonable. Science can begin with speculation—but it doesn’t stay there.",
